\subsection{New families and newer models}
Holding out whole families costs little on the MTEB pools and more on HELM MMLU (Figure~\ref{fig:shift}): at $k=3$ the RMSE of a family-held-out model is 2\% above that of a random-split model on both MTEB pools (0.616 against 0.604; 0.590 against 0.578; the paired difference has an interval including zero on MTEB-Eng and just excluding it on MTEB-Multi) and 14\% above on HELM MMLU (0.534 against 0.467), where 21 families share 79 models, so holding out a family removes a larger part of the history. Predicting models released \emph{later} is harder: on the same test models the RMSE at $k=3$ is 9\% higher than under the grouped split on MTEB-Eng (interval includes zero; 10\% over the grid, interval excludes zero) and 18\% higher on MTEB-Multi (14\% over the grid). The unpaired time-forward RMSEs (0.729 and 0.740) are on later test models with less history and are an upper estimate. The advantage of greedy selection over random does not shrink under shift (gaps $-0.041$ and $-0.086$ on the time-forward split, against $-0.038$ and $-0.076$ grouped; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:robust}). Errors are biased against the strongest models: at $k=3$ the strongest quintile of MTEB-Eng models is predicted too low by 0.10 [$0.02$, $0.19$] standard deviations under the grouped split and 0.31 [$0.16$, $0.47$] under the time-forward split (MTEB-Multi: 0.09 and 0.16), and the weakest HELM MMLU models are predicted too high by 0.14 [$0.00$, $0.24$]. Quintiles formed on the mean score, which contains the targets, show some bias even for a correct model (0.04 in simulation); stratifying on the observed scores leaves 0.06 (MTEB-Eng, interval includes zero) and 0.07 (MTEB-Multi) and none on HELM MMLU; the time-forward values are not decomposed.

