\subsection{Does the closed-form risk track the curve?}
With the true parameters on synthetic data the formula~\eqref{eq:risk} is exact up to Monte Carlo error (median relative gap of the reducible part 0.5\%, maximum 1.5\%; Figure~\ref{fig:theory}, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:theory}). With parameters fitted to $M=60$ to 2\,000 history models the median gap is 26.7\% to 1.5\%, falling roughly like $1/M$, which is the estimation noise of $W$ and $D$ that the formula ignores; the median meets the 10\% tolerance from $M=250$. On the real data (MTEB-Multi and HELM MMLU in the logit-transformed space) the median gap is 36\% (MTEB-Multi), 47\% (MTEB-Eng) and 69\% (HELM MMLU): H4 fails, and the pre-specified tolerance of 15\% is far from met. Most of the gap is a near-constant offset of about 0.08 in squared standardised units, consistent with misspecification, which we did not test; without it the median gap in the $k$-dependent part is 8.5\% (MTEB-Eng), 3.2\% (MTEB-Multi) and 23\% (HELM MMLU), and predicted and measured curves correlate above 0.997.

Appendix Table~\ref{tab:coherence} asks whether low coherence explains why random selection is close to greedy; it does not: the coherence of the fitted loadings is about 3--4 on all three pools, near the low end of our synthetic range of 3--10, yet greedy A removes 35--61\% of the reducible risk of random selection at $k=1,3,10$ and on the MTEB pools the relative gap does not shrink up to $k=10$ (the absolute gap does); across synthetic coherence levels the gap does not vary monotonically (at $k=3$: 51, 53, 66 and 50\%). The small gap in RMSE (6--11\%) arises because the reducible part is a small share of the total error, which includes the floor. The sampled submodularity ratio is 0.97--0.99 on the pools (median over fits; individual fits 0.89--1.00), an upper bound on the true ratio, so we claim no greedy guarantee.
